<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<ecore:EPackage xmi:version="2.0" xmlns:xmi="http://www.omg.org/XMI" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:ecore="http://www.eclipse.org/emf/2002/Ecore" name="alm" nsURI="http://alm/1.0.0" nsPrefix="alm">
  <eAnnotations source="http://www.eclipse.org/emf/2002/GenModel">
    <details key="documentation" value="Agnostic metamodel for the multi-agent learning system (ALM 1.0.0, 2026-10-27). Provide the Lead with a stable view of outcomes, knowledge, activities, resources, roles, evidence and decisions for planning and synthesizing learning tasks in any subject."/>
  </eAnnotations>
  <eAnnotations source="http://alm/annotation">
    <details key="metamodelId" value="ALM"/>
    <details key="metamodelVersion" value="1.0.0"/>
    <details key="source" value="docs/resources/metamodello-agnostico-apprendimento.json"/>
    <details key="referenceSemantics" value="Every entity reference is conceptually represented by EntityRef, with type, id and exact version. Cyclic reference structures are allowed unless prohibited by a specific rule."/>
  </eAnnotations>
  <eAnnotations source="http://alm/annotation/rules">
    <details key="R01" value="immutability and layers: The metamodel is fixed. Subject models and state may vary within policy limits; no proposal may change types, categories or invariants during execution. A DRAFT/PREPARATION context may precede the first model, provided identity and policy come from trusted configuration. A LEARNING/ACTIVE context cannot have empty models."/>
    <details key="R02" value="identity and references: Every entity has an id, version and provenance. Every reference resolves an object of the expected type and exact version; session references remain within the authorized context. DomainModel.definitions may reference only domain_configuration definitions. Loading follows the relevant reference closure, including historical versions needed for evidence and decisions."/>
    <details key="R03" value="agnosticism and roles: Topics, classifications, scales, contexts and strategies are model data. Human roles, worker roles and control components remain distinct."/>
    <details key="R04" value="topic hierarchy: The parentTopics relationship has no cycles. Associative relationships may be cyclic and do not imply prerequisites."/>
    <details key="R05" value="prerequisites: A MANDATORY prerequisite must be SATISFIED according to a defined rule before the dependent activity. UNKNOWN does not count as SATISFIED. RECOMMENDED and RECOMMENDED_SEQUENCE produce findings; CO_REQUISITE requires a joint satisfaction policy. Obligation cycles that prevent starting are invalid."/>
    <details key="R06" value="path composition: LearningDesign.root is a PHASE. A ready PHASE contains at least one child and no direct activities; a ready UNIT contains at least one activity and no children. The hierarchy is acyclic, with one parent per node in the path. Composition and completion conditions are explicit before automatic advancement. Node closure uses the relevant ActivityOccurrence states and its rule; COMPLETED does not prove acquisition and SKIPPED does not count as completion unless an explicit authorized rule says so. A finished WorkerTask instance does not automatically complete the learner's activity."/>
    <details key="R07" value="coverage and readiness: A ready path covers its outcomes through unit activities. Each assessment covers the outcomes it declares it evaluates. A task covers its goal or declares the missing portion."/>
    <details key="R08" value="resource use: In LEARNING, a resource used must have a valid, approved binding consistent with the context, audience and task. PREPARATION allows candidates within the authorized scope. A mandatory requirement must be satisfied before the activity. Discovering resources outside scope is possible only in a separate authorized PREPARATION context, not as an implicit exception to the LEARNING filter."/>
    <details key="R09" value="sources and provenance: Distinguish source, usage conditions, derived material, citation and claim. Generated resources declare their origin and sources actually used. A search score does not confer reliability or approval."/>
    <details key="R10" value="rubric: Criterion keys are unique within the rubric and level keys within the criterion. Every CriterionJudgment references existing keys in the applied version and relevant evidence. Weights and aggregation are required only when specified by the rule."/>
    <details key="R11" value="evidence: Actual evidence references the person, context, activity, occurrence/attempt, outcomes and versions. Its type must be allowed by the assessment specification. Agent-produced text and technical task success are not themselves proof of learning. The evidence's person, context and activity references must agree with the indicated ActivityOccurrence. Its historical version identifies the attempt even if the occurrence state evolves."/>
    <details key="R12" value="outcome rules: Acquisition or a determined judgment requires defined rules and sufficient inputs. An ambiguous or free-form source description does not automatically become an executable predicate. Missing evidence or criteria produces UNKNOWN/undetermined."/>
    <details key="R13" value="verified state and responsibility: VERIFIED requires ACQUIRED according to criteria, admissible evidence, a satisfied verificationRule, formal evaluation and confirmation by an authorized human. ACQUIRED may be recorded with an approved rule and existing authorization without equating to verification."/>
    <details key="R14" value="least privilege: Required task capabilities ⊆ capabilities authorized by profile and context. Capabilities actually exposed to the worker ⊆ required capabilities. Tools, APIs, KBs and data follow the same scope constraint."/>
    <details key="R15" value="access and isolation: Authorization for mediated retrieval does not automatically expose a tool to the worker. No worker inherits rights from the Lead, previous tasks or retrieved sources; reads are limited to necessary context."/>
    <details key="R16" value="role and output: Each specialization has one category and one role from the closed taxonomy. Task intent, output and topic are consistent with that profile. The Socratic constraint permits neither direct nor indirectly revealed solutions, including in synthesis."/>
    <details key="R17" value="plan: Steps have unique IDs, existing agents and dependencies within the same plan, without self-dependencies or cycles. Application control verifies limits and permissions and applies stable sequential topological ordering."/>
    <details key="R18" value="outcomes and coverage: An unsuccessful dependency prevents the dependent step. A nonempty plan with no successes ends in error. Synthesis uses only usable outputs and explicitly declares partial coverage. SUCCEEDED in the final workflow also requires per-step semantic review; completion of the call alone is insufficient. The check occurs before output may become input to a dependent task."/>
    <details key="R19" value="proposals and decisions: A conforming proposal remains a proposal until the required decision. Only an authorized responsible person may approve it; approval and application are distinct events. A revision request entails a new version to review. The decision references the version actually reviewed and its reviewedPayloadKey. A new revision adding only status or a decision link retains approval if substantive content is unchanged. Application control verifies content identity without trusting a key declared by the agent. A ChangeProposal decision may also approve bindings or evaluations included in the candidate: their reviewPayloadKey must appear in coveredPayloadKeys and be part of the content actually reviewed. An object added or modified after review does not inherit proposal approval."/>
    <details key="R20" value="state consistency: Apply a change only if authorization, base versions, references and constraints remain valid. Record the cause, author/controller, decision or policy, previous revision and next revision. Do not reinterpret historical evidence with a new rubric. Audit events alone or administrative proposal updates do not change the revision of the authoritative state on which it is based. Substantive changes to that context instead require a base check. The history of an approved evaluation or binding remains reconstructable after later administrative revisions."/>
    <details key="R21" value="final response: The response distinguishes grounded content, explicit inference, gaps and proposals. It reports sources actually used when content is source-based; preserves the educational role and does not claim unapplied updates."/>
  </eAnnotations>
